00:00:04.620 --> 00:00:08.570
Kerry: If I think about the young people
I support at the moment, so the three that

00:00:08.570 --> 00:00:12.650
are in my class, I have a wide variety.

00:00:12.650 --> 00:00:15.440
There may only be three, but
they are each individual people.

00:00:15.770 --> 00:00:20.210
So one young person, if it's
verbal instruction needs the

00:00:20.210 --> 00:00:22.650
visual support to go along with it.

00:00:22.680 --> 00:00:25.770
And if there's any verbal, it should
be a maximum of three words at a time.

00:00:26.370 --> 00:00:29.880
And then at least 30 seconds
worth of silent processing

00:00:29.880 --> 00:00:31.260
time to go along with that.

00:00:32.670 --> 00:00:39.470
For another young person
I support there's...

00:00:40.800 --> 00:00:43.410
they can take on a lot
more verbal information.

00:00:44.069 --> 00:00:48.319
In fact, they quite like the intricacies
of why they need to be doing XYZ in this

00:00:48.319 --> 00:00:52.949
lesson, and like to know the reason behind
everything because that helps them feel

00:00:52.949 --> 00:00:56.580
calm and centered, so they can take on
a lot of verbal information, but it has

00:00:56.580 --> 00:01:01.060
to be relevant to that moment in time.

00:01:02.091 --> 00:01:08.300
And then the other young person I
support, again, is okay with verbal

00:01:08.300 --> 00:01:10.950
instruction to a two-step level.

00:01:12.600 --> 00:01:17.760
But does like it backed up with visual
supports and again, given time to

00:01:17.760 --> 00:01:21.720
process it and do it in their own time.

00:01:21.720 --> 00:01:23.280
So not to be rushed through.

00:01:24.045 --> 00:01:28.725
Zoe: Certain turns of phrase and things
like sarcasm, the child or young person

00:01:28.725 --> 00:01:32.355
may not be able to interpret that as
sarcasm they may take it very literally.

00:01:32.625 --> 00:01:35.715
The other thing is if you're
trying to explain something in

00:01:35.715 --> 00:01:37.335
general is to use visual prompts.

00:01:37.355 --> 00:01:40.414
So it makes more sense to
the child or young person.

00:01:40.625 --> 00:01:46.955
Ellen: If I had a student that would
come into IT, if I gave them a lot

00:01:46.955 --> 00:01:53.273
of paper tasks on their desks, they
might not know when the tasks end.

00:01:54.585 --> 00:01:58.095
And they might look at it and think
like "that's a lot, I'm not doing that".

00:01:58.365 --> 00:02:04.845
So the way I can make it easier is by
presenting like one page at a time.

00:02:05.525 --> 00:02:12.515
Phil: I work with a lot of young people
who either need more time to process

00:02:12.605 --> 00:02:19.710
verbal information and or can be
overloaded with too much verbal input.

00:02:20.250 --> 00:02:23.910
Always remember that, you know, chances
are, they're not going to be ignoring

00:02:23.940 --> 00:02:26.250
you if, you know, they know the process.

00:02:26.280 --> 00:02:30.510
And that can be difficult because
some people think that, you know, the

00:02:30.510 --> 00:02:32.130
autistic individual is being rude.

00:02:32.160 --> 00:02:36.740
But you know, if you have that patience
and awareness, so ask a question,

00:02:36.950 --> 00:02:42.470
count to 10, give them time to process,
ask again, reduce your language.

00:02:44.010 --> 00:02:47.130
Zoe T: Verbal communication has
to be seen in the context of

00:02:47.130 --> 00:02:52.650
human communication, which is all
about emotions and intentions.

00:02:53.100 --> 00:02:57.060
And in order to help people with
autism better understand verbal

00:02:57.060 --> 00:03:00.780
communication, that is where the
focus needs to be in my view.

00:03:00.810 --> 00:03:06.960
So, it would be about using the
relationship between the person

00:03:06.960 --> 00:03:11.070
with autism and the person, the
neuro-typical person, using the

00:03:11.070 --> 00:03:15.900
relationship between those people
to help that person with autism,

00:03:15.900 --> 00:03:17.550
without putting any pressure on them.

